  "worked_solution": "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=\u27e80,\\,0,\\,-1\u27e9.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =\u27e8x,\\,y,\\,x+2y\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80,\\,0,\\,-1\u27e9 \\\\  & =-(x+2y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{S}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2xy){|}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{y}{2}+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
